<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Student materials on Llumàtics</title><link>https://112books.github.io/llumatics-web/en/privat/</link><description>Recent content in Student materials on Llumàtics</description><generator>Hugo</generator><language>en</language><atom:link href="https://112books.github.io/llumatics-web/en/privat/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>B/W Film Development</title><link>https://112books.github.io/llumatics-web/en/tallers/revelat-bn/privat/doc/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://112books.github.io/llumatics-web/en/tallers/revelat-bn/privat/doc/</guid><description>&lt;h2 id="introduction"&gt;Introduction&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Black and white film development is the first step towards total control of the photographic process. Unlike colour developing, B/W is robust, forgiving and gives you enormous room to experiment.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In this workshop you have learned that there is no &amp;ldquo;correct&amp;rdquo; development — there is the development that best suits your photographic intention.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id="history"&gt;History&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Photographic developing as we know it today has its roots in the 19th century. Fox Talbot described in 1839 the basic principle: light-sensitive silver salts are reduced to metallic silver in the presence of a reducing agent (the developer). Since then, the chemistry has evolved but the principle has not changed.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Introduction to Analogue Photography</title><link>https://112books.github.io/llumatics-web/en/tallers/fonaments-iniciacio-puntual/privat/doc/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://112books.github.io/llumatics-web/en/tallers/fonaments-iniciacio-puntual/privat/doc/</guid><description>&lt;h2 id="what-is-analogue-photography"&gt;What is analogue photography?&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Analogue photography — also known as film photography, traditional photography, silver-based photography or chemical photography — is the term used to describe the traditional photographic process, as distinct from the more recently developed digital photography. It is based on a physico-chemical process involving the use of photosensitive material (applied to glass plates, flexible film, or translucent material) and its stabilisation (development), in order to obtain and process images.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Introduction to Film Development</title><link>https://112books.github.io/llumatics-web/en/tallers/iniciacio-revelat/privat/doc/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://112books.github.io/llumatics-web/en/tallers/iniciacio-revelat/privat/doc/</guid><description>&lt;h2 id="materials-checklist"&gt;Materials checklist&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Before developing, make sure you have everything you need:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Equipment&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Developing tank (Patterson, Jobo or similar) + reel(s)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;35mm film cartridge opener: a dedicated opener, a bottle opener, or simply forcing it open&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Accurate thermometer (±0.5 °C) — fever thermometers don&amp;rsquo;t work; they start at too high a temperature&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Graduated measuring cylinder (500 ml or 1,000 ml)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Scissors&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Funnel&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Timer or phone&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Opaque bottles for solutions&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Chemistry&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Developer (Rodinal, HC-110, ID-11, Caffenol…)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Stop bath (diluted acetic or citric acid) — we won&amp;rsquo;t be using this in the workshop&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Fixer (Ilford Rapid Fixer, Kodak Fixer)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Wetting agent (Kodak Photo-Flo, Ilford Ilfotol)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Optional but useful&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Portrait in Large Format</title><link>https://112books.github.io/llumatics-web/en/tallers/retrat-gran-format/privat/doc/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://112books.github.io/llumatics-web/en/tallers/retrat-gran-format/privat/doc/</guid><description>&lt;h2 id="what-is-large-format-photography"&gt;What is large format photography&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Large format photography is made with specialist cameras that produce a negative (or positive) far larger than 35mm or medium format. It begins at 9×12 cm plates, continues through the classic 4×5 inches (10×12.7 cm), 13×18 cm and 20×25 cm, and extends to even larger sizes, often with custom-built cameras.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;One of the primary reasons to shoot large format is the &lt;strong&gt;overwhelming image quality&lt;/strong&gt;: a 4×5&amp;quot; negative has twenty times more emulsion area than a 35mm negative. This translates into extraordinary detail, a very rich tonal scale, and the ability to make enormous enlargements with no visible loss of quality.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Student materials — Artisanal Developers</title><link>https://112books.github.io/llumatics-web/en/tallers/reveladors-artesanals/privat/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://112books.github.io/llumatics-web/en/tallers/reveladors-artesanals/privat/</guid><description/></item><item><title>Student materials — B/W Film Development</title><link>https://112books.github.io/llumatics-web/en/tallers/revelat-bn/privat/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://112books.github.io/llumatics-web/en/tallers/revelat-bn/privat/</guid><description/></item><item><title>Student materials — Beers Developer</title><link>https://112books.github.io/llumatics-web/en/tallers/copies-beers-developer/privat/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://112books.github.io/llumatics-web/en/tallers/copies-beers-developer/privat/</guid><description/></item><item><title>Student materials — Cianotípia</title><link>https://112books.github.io/llumatics-web/en/tallers/cianotipia/privat/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://112books.github.io/llumatics-web/en/tallers/cianotipia/privat/</guid><description/></item><item><title>Student materials — Còpies en paper</title><link>https://112books.github.io/llumatics-web/en/tallers/copies-en-paper/privat/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://112books.github.io/llumatics-web/en/tallers/copies-en-paper/privat/</guid><description/></item><item><title>Student materials — Del negatiu a la imatge — edició digital per a fotografia a